---
// What the game, the room server and this site send and keep - the page
// App Store Connect's Privacy Policy URL points at. Keep it to what the code
// does: the progress store (src/levels.rs, src/mapstore.rs), the device
// token and nickname (site/src/scripts/room.ts, src/net/client.rs), the
// room server's log lines (server/src/room.rs) and the newsletter form
// (site/src/site.ts). A change to any of them changes this page and its
// date.
import Page from "../layouts/Page.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL } from "../site";
---

<Page title="Privacy" description="What BongBong sends, what it keeps, and where." band="archipelago" current="privacy" credit>
    <h1>Privacy</h1>
    <p class="updated">Last updated 15 October 2026</p>

    <div class="short">
        <p class="pixel label">The short version</p>
        <p>BongBong has no accounts, no ads, no analytics and no tracking. Your progress and the maps you build stay on
            your device. Playing online sends the room server only what it needs to run the round.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>On your device</h2>
    <p>The levels you have cleared and the maps you build are saved where you play: in the app's own storage on iPhone,
        iPad, Android, macOS, Windows and Linux, and in your browser's storage on bongbong.io. They are never
        uploaded.</p>
    <p>On bongbong.io the browser also keeps a random id it makes up on your first visit, and one for each tab, so
        that reloading the page during an online round puts you back in your own seat. They are sent only when you
        play online.</p>

    <h2>Playing online</h2>
    <p>When you host or join a co-op room, the room server at rooms.bongbong.io receives what it needs to run the
        round for everyone in it:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>your nickname, which is an anonymous <span class="pixel nick">Player #XXXXXX</span> made from the random
            id above, unless you start the desktop game with a nickname of your own;
        </li>
        <li>that random id, so a dropped connection gets your seat back;</li>
        <li>the room code;</li>
        <li>your tank's controls while the round runs.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The server keeps the round in memory and lets it go when the room closes. Its log notes each room it opens,
        with the room's code and map, and each player who joins or leaves it, with their nickname. Like any server, it
        sees your IP address while you are connected.</p>

    <h2>Updates by email</h2>
    <p>If you subscribe, your email address goes to Kit, which keeps it to send you a note when a new version ships.
        Every email has an unsubscribe link. Nothing of Kit's loads with the game: your browser talks to Kit only when
        you press Subscribe. <a href="https://kit.com/privacy">Kit's privacy policy</a></p>

    <h2>This website</h2>
    <p>bongbong.io is served by Cloudflare, which sees your IP address and browser in order to deliver the page. The
        page sets no cookies of its own and loads nothing from other sites: the game, its pictures and its fonts all
        come from bongbong.io.</p>

    <h2>Questions</h2>
    <p>BongBong is made by Oto Brglez. Write to <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>. If anything
        here changes, this page changes with it, and so does its date.</p>
</Page>

